{"hash":"c48b7adec937ed5c","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":112.122,"said":"You stand at the brass rail, at the threshold of Shri Ramanathaswamy. Look: the linga is small, the linga that Sita shaped from the sand of the shore, adorned today in gold beneath a five-hooded serpent, heavy with jasmine and roses, lit by brass lamps. Come very early, around five in the morning, and you may see the Spatika linga darshan, when a small linga of clear crystal is bathed and worshipped here before the day begins. And this is where the long pilgrimage ends. The sand you saw taken from Agni Theertham goes north to Kashi and is given to the Ganga; the pilgrim fills a vessel with Ganga water, carries it all the way back, and pours it over Ramanathaswamy in abhishekam. By tradition, only then is the Kashi yatra complete. Fold your hands. Adi Shankaracharya sings of this place in the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram: ‘Sutamraparni jalarashiyoge nibadhya setum vishikhair asankhyaih, Shri Ramachandrena samarpitam tam Rameshvarakhyam niyatam namami.’ ‘Where the Tamraparni meets the ocean, Shri Ramachandra bound a bridge with countless arrows and offered this Lord his worship: to him, named Rameshwara, I bow, always.’ Rameshwaram is the seventh Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Let the nadaswaram and the bells begin, and let the lamps be raised. Om Namah Shivaya.\n\nDid you know? In 2021 the Sringeri Sharada Peetham offered a crystal linga to the temple, and its daily abhishekam around 5 AM began the next day.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1486},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1502,"end":1634}],"words":[[0.098,0.192,0,3],[0.302,0.481,4,9],[0.795,0.108,10,12],[0.915,0.108,13,16],[1.036,0.409,17,22],[1.456,0.325,23,27],[1.985,0.12,29,31],[2.117,0.108,32,35],[2.